WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.150
Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.660
I'm Gagandeep Singh and I'm
joined by my colleague Xu

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.300
Fei. We are Solutions
Engineers at RStudio,

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.010
soon to be called Posit.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.840
And we are here to
answer the question

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.180
that we get asked a
lot, which is can you

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.100
use Python with RStudio Team?

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.590
And the answer is, of
course, yes you can.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.840
Very recently I had to
answer this question,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.540
so I'm going to start by
sharing a story with you.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:28.927
I'm an Indian National.

00:00:28.927 --> 00:00:31.260
In order to be here in the
US to attend this conference,

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.680
I needed to get a visa.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.500
And as part of the
immigration process,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.360
I have to get an
interview with an officer

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.710
where they ask you questions
like, where are you going?

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:40.230
What will you be doing?

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.150
So I told them that I'm
going to this conference.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.880
I'm giving a talk about
using Python with RStudio.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.940
The officer said,
oh, I know Python,

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:53.130
but I didn't know you could
use it with RStudio.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:56.580
I told him that's exactly
what my talk is about.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.500
Unfortunately, Officer
Johnson could not

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.610
be here with us today.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:02.640
But if you are in
the audience, we

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.860
are assuming that you
are a multi either

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.890
you are a multilingual data
scientist who has access

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.160
to RStudio's pro products.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.800
And wants to use
Python with them

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.530
or you are an R user
who works with a lot

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:16.555
of Python colleagues,
and you may or may not

00:01:16.555 --> 00:01:18.930
have access to our products,
but you're looking for ways

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.430
to collaborate with them.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.470
So it is one thing
to say that you can

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.010
use Python in RStudio products.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.630
We're here to show you how
to work with the use case.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:32.110
We're in the DC area
right now and there

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.080
is an active bikeshare
program outside called Capital

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:35.980
Bikeshare.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.650
We went out and took
a picture yesterday

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:42.262
and this is the station
right outside of the hotel.

00:01:42.262 --> 00:01:43.720
As you can see that
there are bikes

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.810
docked into the bike station.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.180
And when you go to
the company's website,

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.743
you will see a map that shows
you all the bike stations.

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:52.410
When you click on one
of those stations,

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.930
you will show you how many
bikes are available right now

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.290
from a live data feed.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.630
Now, what it doesn't
tell you is how many

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:02.150
bikes are going to be
available in the future.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:05.060
As we know, the
bike availability

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:09.229
varies a lot by the time
of day, the location.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.780
And if we want to have a
smooth trip experience,

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.540
we want to be able to predict
how many bikes in the future.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:18.110
So because there is
a live data feed,

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.350
we can actually grab the
data, store it somewhere

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:22.772
and make a predictive model.

00:02:22.772 --> 00:02:24.980
So that we can actually know
how many bikes are going

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.250
to be available in the future.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.860
So that's we want to do.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.980
If you were here at the
conference back in 2020,

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:35.660
you probably have seen a talk
that uses the similar data set,

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.600
that does everything in R packages.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.710
It's actually from
our colleague Alex.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:43.220
And so this project has
been successfully running

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:44.310
for over two years.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.820
So a lot of data has
been processed and stored

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.520
as interoperable data assets
that we'll get back to

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:50.340
in a second.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.690
There's a lot of data
being stored and processed.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:55.670
When I joined the
company last year

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.320
from more of a
Python data science

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.910
background and Gagan
and I started talking.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:03.180
So since we have all these data,
can we do something different?

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.420
Can we do something
entirely in Python and using

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.610
our pro products?

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:10.710
And we wanted to do
this for two reasons.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.990
Number one, well,
just because we can.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:14.300
Why not?

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.290
Well, that's not
really a good reason.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.350
The second reason is
actually we thought

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:21.080
this could be a very
common scenario where

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:25.280
in our pro product customers,
the teams that are working one

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.380
language may want to
collaborate with another team

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.930
in a different language
using interoperable data

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:34.190
assets that's completely
deployed in the pro product

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:36.070
ecosystem.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:41.050
So we are also thinking that
the conference is in DC,

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.840
so we actually get to go
to DC and write those bikes

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.420
and here we are.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:49.870
So let's recap the workflow for
the bike prediction use case.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:52.600
As in with any other project,

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.000
it starts with the data
source, which in this case

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.250
is the Capital Bike
Share API and the data

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:59.080
is refreshed every day.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:02.020
Then the objective is
to import this data,

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.930
transform it, and save
it either in the database

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.660
or an interoperable
data asset like pins.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.060
So based on the
frequency of the update,

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.950
then we have to
consume this data,

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:13.810
build our prediction
model to show

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:15.890
the number of bikes
for each bike station.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:19.029
Then we have to move this
model to a deployable location

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:21.370
where the output, the
predictions of number of bikes

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:23.780
can be shared with
other applications.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.510
And speaking of
other applications,

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:30.400
we have to build a way to share
the predictions with our users.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.760
As in the previous
talk, this work is already

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:38.367
available and running
successfully all in R. Xu Fei

00:04:38.367 --> 00:04:40.450
and I well, when we were
looking at this use case,

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:43.030
we realized that we
have access to this data

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:46.150
already in the form of
the database and the pins

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.620
that the R ETL
process is updating.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.740
So we decided to,
in order to address

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:54.005
the needs of a team which
uses a similar workflow, where

00:04:54.005 --> 00:04:55.630
part of the workflow
is in one language

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:57.640
and part of the workflow
is in another language,

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.560
we decided that we'll
build the model,

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:04.530
build the dashboards in Python
all within our RStudio products.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:07.290
So let's take a look at
the application itself.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:10.770
So this is developed
in Shiny for Python

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.468
as you heard yesterday
deployed on RStudio

00:05:13.468 --> 00:05:15.510
Connect which we will get
into the second you see

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.850
a map of the DC
area and there are

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.330
circles with numbers in them.

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:22.420
These represented this
cluster of bike stations.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:23.940
If you click into
it, it zooms in

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.148
and eventually we'll see
blue circles that represents

00:05:26.148 --> 00:05:27.395
the stations themselves.

00:05:27.395 --> 00:05:29.770
The bigger the circle, the
more bikes that are available.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:31.470
And if you click on
one of the circles,

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.840
it's going to give you
the predictive graph

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.380
of the number of bikes
available in the next 24 hours.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:43.390
So you heard of RStudio Team and
RStudio professional products

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:46.730
and what are they
and why do we need them,

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:48.970
and how does Python fit in here?

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:51.280
RStudio Team is
actually an umbrella term

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:55.090
for three professional products,
namely Workbench, Connect

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:56.913
and Package Manager.

00:05:56.913 --> 00:05:58.330
As Python data
scientists, we need

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:01.000
a place to start writing
our project and code

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.740
and we need an
environment to code.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:05.290
And so Workbench offers
a centralized, server

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:07.030
based environment
that allows you

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:10.040
to code in your favorite
data science IDEs,

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:14.260
so Jupyter and VS Code.
As your project matures,

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:16.960
you may want to start
sharing your content

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.350
and deploy to somewhere.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.700
And that's where
your RStudio Connect comes in,

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:23.450
it's designed
exactly for that.

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:26.890
And it allows you to
deploy a very wide variety

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:29.740
of data, data science
assets, especially

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.980
the interoperable ones
such as pins, APIs

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:36.220
and reports like Quarto
and Jupyter Notebooks

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:40.010
and interactive dashboards
like Plotly Dash, Streamlit,

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:44.230
and of course, our favorite and
the latest Shiny for Python.

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:46.300
And if you want to
install packages

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.630
into a centralized location
and that's not directly

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:54.510
from PyPi, you can use
Package Manager for that.

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:56.970
So what are what are
we going to show you

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:58.530
in the next few
minutes is that we're

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:01.380
going to show you a workflow
from developing the model using

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:05.410
existing interoperable data
objects in the Workbench.

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:08.220
And we deploy that to connect
and come back and visit

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.670
the application itself.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:11.800
We'll make a change
and we'll update it

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:15.120
live to the Connect deployment.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:17.580
So let's see the demo.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:21.005
As Xu Fei mentioned, we're
going to start with we already

00:07:21.005 --> 00:07:22.380
have access to
the data, so we're

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:24.990
going to start with the
model building process.

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:26.610
So in order for me
to start working,

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:28.120
I need access to the IDE.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:31.710
So I go to our demo
Workbench and as Xu Fei said,

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:35.020
it supports multiple IDEs.
A controversial opinion,

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:37.057
I like to start my
work in notebooks,

00:07:37.057 --> 00:07:39.640
especially when I'm doing model
building and data exploration.

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.585
So I'm going to choose
the IDE Jupyter Lab here

00:07:42.585 --> 00:07:44.710
and then I'm going to launch
a Jupyter Lab session.

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:46.830
I've already launched
it here, and this

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:50.220
is the notebook I'm using to
train the model and deploy it.

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.760
And as you can see,
I might have to zoom

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.390
in a bit, a little bit more.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.650
Cool So I'm beginning
with all the basics,

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:05.020
starting with importing all
the packages that I need

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:07.300
in my work, setting
about my environment,

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:10.050
providing API keys and
database passwords.

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:12.370
I've also built some
functions for repeatable code

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:14.290
that I'm going to
use later, and this

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:16.000
is where I start
making a connection

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:19.030
to the database, which has all
the data about the bike stored.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:22.030
So this database is being
updated by the R ETL process,

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:24.970
so I don't need to do the
data cleaning and exploration.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:28.090
I can directly import this data
as a data frame in my Python

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:28.850
environment.

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:30.850
And I can create a testing
and training data set

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:32.409
and start building my model.

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:36.429
So for this work, we chose
the random forest model,

00:08:36.429 --> 00:08:38.442
but like another
iteration of this work

00:08:38.442 --> 00:08:40.150
can be building
different kinds of models

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:41.750
and comparing the performances.

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:43.970
But let's take the random
forest for this one.

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:45.590
And now I build the model.

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:46.580
I need to test it.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:49.630
So I create my testing set
and I compare the results

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:51.460
with the actual data.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.030
What I have done here

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:56.240
so far is what we do every
day as data scientists.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:59.140
Right? and maybe you all
can do it better than I did.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:02.830
But the next challenge for
me is to move this model out

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:04.780
of my notebook
into a place where

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:08.110
other applications can consume
the predictions it's making.

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:10.990
And this is where I'm going to
use a combination of vetiver

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:12.200
and RStudio Connect.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.880
So as Julia introduced vetiver
in her keynote yesterday

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.610
and also Isabel gave a
great talk about using vetiver

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:22.090
in ML Ops operations, I'm going
to skip explaining vetiver,

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:25.420
but I'm going to show you
how I use it in my work.

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:28.990
So in order to start working, I
will convert the random forest

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:31.300
model that I just built
into a vetiver model

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:33.280
so that vetiver can
interact with it.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:37.030
And once I've converted that,
I will pin this vetiver model

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:39.200
onto an RSConnect board.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:41.110
Yes, this is the
same pins package

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:43.900
that is available in R, but now
it's also available in Python

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:46.580
and it's used in vetiver
for model versioning.

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:50.110
And once I've pinned the
model onto RStudio Connect,

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:54.380
I will deploy it as a Fast API
endpoint onto Connect itself.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:56.140
And I'm using the
in-built function

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:58.280
from vetiver called
vetiver_deploy_rsconnect.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:00.070
So what this
function is doing is

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:02.980
I'm passing the name of the
pin directly to the function

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:05.320
and it's converting this
function into a Fast API,

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:07.810
converting this
model into a Fast API

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:09.320
and directly deploying it on

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:10.820
my RStudio Connect instance.

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:14.410
So you can see right? Like
from a few lines of code

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:16.840
under 10 lines of code,
I was able to move

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:21.880
this model from my notebook into
Connect using vetiver.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:24.350
So I've done the deployment
to save us time here.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:26.240
Let's see what the
deployment looks like.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.260
So this is my deployed
model as a Fast API

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:30.740
on to RStudio Connect.

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:34.910
So I get access to these docs
that the Fast API provides.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:38.690
I can see the API URL, I can
see what kind of features

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:39.290
it's taking.

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:42.760
I can make a sample prediction,
I can play with it too.

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:45.830
As this is API is running
on RStudio Connect,

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:47.860
I get access to
all these features,

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:50.650
which is what Connect
provides so I can decide who

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:52.490
I want to share this API with.

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:54.890
I can change the runtime
credentials for this API.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:57.530
I can manage it here.

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:01.370
What I've done so
far is run my run

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:04.440
the model once on the current
state of the database.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:08.420
But the R process is updating
this database every day

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:11.100
because the number of bikes
are changing every day.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:13.130
So that data is also
getting refreshed.

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:16.830
So my next task is to update
my model with the latest data.

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:20.330
So that it's always available
with the right data,

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:21.980
the current data.

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:25.978
So for this work, I'm also
again using RStudio Connect.

00:11:25.978 --> 00:11:27.770
RStudio Connect
provides you the ability

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:30.390
to deploy a Jupyter
Notebook and schedule it.

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:32.810
So I'm going to deploy
this Jupyter Notebook using

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:35.150
the Git-based deployment
in RStudio Connect.

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:37.410
This is one of the many types
of deployment available.

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:38.993
I'm going to use Git
based to make sure

00:11:38.993 --> 00:11:41.780
that my code on my Git repo
